\paragraph{(h) Per-mutation scaling patterns.}
Scaling benefits are highly non-uniform across mutation types (Appendix, Table~\ref{tab:mutation_breakdown}). \texttt{off\_by\_one} is the largest \cls{} scaling beneficiary: accuracy jumps from 58.5\% (4B) to 83.0\% (8B mean, $+$24.6\pp{}). In contrast, \texttt{boundary\_removal} is scale-invariant at ${\sim}$46\% across all seeds and sizes, identifying a difficulty ceiling independent of capacity. For \cwm{}, gold mutation parse rate collapses from 43.5\% (4B) to 23.9\% (8B) despite being the simplest target---all tests pass by construction---indicating that generation capability degrades even for trivially correct code at larger scale.

\paragraph{(i) Inference efficiency.}
\cls{} achieves 217$\times$ higher throughput than \cwm{} at repo level (853 vs.\ 3.9 inferences/min; Qwen3-4B, RTX 5090, 100 samples, avg ${\sim}$1,040 input tokens). The speedup derives directly from the formulation: classification requires a single output token, whereas \cwm{} generates an average of 477 tokens per inference. Median latency is 48ms (\cls{}) vs.\ 16.3s (\cwm{}). Moreover, 73.8\% of \cwm{}'s generated output at 8B is unparseable (\S\ref{sec:repo}), meaning the majority of generation budget is spent producing tokens that cannot yield a valid verdict. Combined with higher accuracy (83.63\% vs.\ 58.04\%) and perfect format compliance (0\% vs.\ 53.7\% unparseable at 4B), classification provides a triple advantage at repository-level complexity: accuracy, reliability, and throughput.

